    <para id="para-00120">Umaña-Taylor, A. J., Douglass, S., Updegraff, K. A., &amp; Marsiglia, F. F. (2018). A small-scale randomized efficacy trial of the Identity Project: Promoting adolescents’ ethnic–racial identity exploration and resolution. Child Development, 89(3), 862–870. https://doi.org/10.1111/cdev.12755</para>
    <para id="para-00121">Walters KL. Urban American Indian identity attitudes and acculturation styles. Journal of Human Behavior in the Social Environment. 1999;2(1–2):163–178.</para>
    <para id="para-00122">Woo, B., Maglalang, D. D., Ko, S., Park, M., Choi, Y., &amp; Takeuchi, D. T. (2020). Racial discrimination, ethnic-racial socialization, and cultural identities among Asian American youths. Cultural Diversity and Ethnic Minority Psychology, 26(4), 447. https://psycnet.apa.org/manuscript/2020-13975-001.pdf</para>
    <para id="para-00123">Worthington, R. L., Savoy, H. B., Dillon, F. R., &amp; Vernaglia, E. R. (2002). Heterosexual Identity Development: A Multidimensional Model of Individual and Social Identity. <emphasis effect="italics">The Counseling Psychologist, 30</emphasis>(4), 496-531.</para>
    <para id="para-00124">Ybarra, M. L., Rosario, M., Saewyc, E., &amp; Goodenow, C. (2016). Sexual behaviors and partner characteristics by sexual identity among adolescent girls. <emphasis effect="italics">Journal of Adolescent Health, 58</emphasis>(3), 310-316.</para>
    </section>
    </content>
    <glossary>
    <definition id="def-00001">
    <term>code switching</term>
    <meaning>ability to change our perspective and behavior to match the demands of our current cultural environment</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00002">
    <term>colorblindness</term>
    <meaning>approach that focuses on the similarities among ethnoracial groups, often as a claim to not recognize either positive or negative qualities based on ethnicity or race</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00003">
    <term>cultural heritage</term>
    <meaning>adaptations to the natural and social world that have shared meaning and are passed from generation to generation</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00004">
    <term>gender identity</term>
    <meaning>someone’s own psychological sense and acceptance of their gender, and includes ideas about masculinity, femininity, non-binary and other dimensions</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00005">
    <term>gender intensification hypothesis</term>
    <meaning>prediction that conforming to gender roles is of heightened relevance during adolescence</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00006">
    <term>gender non-conforming behavior</term>
    <meaning>behavior that does not match society’s expectations for the individual’s assigned gender</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00007">
    <term>heteronormativity</term>
    <meaning>the idea that heterosexuality is the norm or preferred sexual orientation and that opposite-sex people are best suited for sexual and marital relationships</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00008">
    <term>sexual identity</term>
    <meaning>sense of our self as a sexual being</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00009">
    <term>sexual orientation</term>
    <meaning>sex of those to whom we are sexually, emotionally, and/or romantically attracted</meaning>
    </definition>
    </glossary>
    </document>